    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d976">

                <pb facs="1071" n="1059" sameAs="frus:frus1944v03/pg1059"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">865.01/2218a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé at Algiers</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Chapin</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-17T18:00:00-04:00">March 17, 1944—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>797. For Reinhardt. With reference to Department’s earlier telegram quoting
                    President and Prime Minister on the Italian political crisis, replies from the
                    Prime Minister, recently received, to the President’s messages of March 8 and
                    March 13<anchor n="92" xml:id="d976-f1"/> are quoted below:</p>
                <p>[Here follows the first of Churchill’s replies, message No. 618, March 13,
                    printed on <ref target="#pg1043">page 1043</ref>; the second reply is printed
                    below.]</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“My number 618 crossed your number 498.<anchor n="93" xml:id="d976-f2"/> The
                        Russians meanwhile have announced that they have sent to present Italian
                        Government, with whom technically we are still at war, a fully credited
                        Ambassador. Without further consideration, I do not think it would be wise
                        to accede to the proposals of the six parties and demand immediately the
                        abdication of the King and the installation of Signor Croce as Lieutenant of
                        the Realm of Italy. Upon this proposal, which you properly call ‘a major
                        political decision’, I will consult with the War Cabinet. We have suffered
                        232,000 casualties in our war with Italy which has lasted since June 1940,
                        as well as extensive warship losses. That our view in this matter will
                        receive consideration from you, I am sure. Every effort ought to be made by
                        us to act together. Please remember that I made a public commitment on the
                        strength of your number 464<anchor n="94" xml:id="d976-f3"/> and that any
                        divergence cannot possibly avoid notice.”</p>
                </quote>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
